<title>An introduction to discrete dynamical systems</title>
<p><ref>Video 1</ref> | <ref>Video 2</ref> | <ref>Summary</ref> | <ref>Transcripts</ref> </p>

<aside><title>Transcript: An introduction to discrete dynamical systems, part 1</title>
<p>
A dynamical system is about how things change over time. Now, the “things” that change are represented by state variables. The simplest case we are going to consider:  just having one state variable, and let’s let <m>m</m> be the number of moose in a population.</p>
<p>Now, these moose are breeding and dying, so the number changes from year to year. So let’s represent time by a new variable, lets call it <m>t</m>, and <m>t</m> will be time in years. Then, <m>m_t</m>, is the number of moose in year <m>t</m>. <m>m_t</m> is just a single number. Now, these moose could be changing continuously over time, but let’s say that we take a census of the population once a year, a snapshot of the moose population, and let’s say that we have the first snapshot, well that’d be at year zero, and we call that snapshot, <m>m_0</m>, (<m>m</m>-"zero" we’re going to call it) and let’s say that’s about 1000. So, imagine we have 1000 moose in year zero. Since we measure moose only once a year, our model of how the moose population changes over time will be a model of going from one snapshot to another. We’ll need a rule of how to go from the population in year 0, to the population in year 1, and the population from year 1 to year 2, etc. We’ll have to have a rule for all these transitions. Now, since we are using discrete time steps, the resulting dynamical system is a discrete time dynamical system, but since that's too many words for me, I usually just call it a discrete dynamical system (with it being understood that time is what's discrete).</p>
<p>To come up with a rule for how the moose population evolves over time, let's imagine the moose are successful; so that in each year their population increases by 8%. How can we write down this rule mathematically? Well, let's think of what the change means mathematically. The change from, say year 0 to year 1, well, what is that change? Well, we have to take the population size in year 1, <m>m_1</m>, subtract off the population of what it was originally, <m>m_0</m>, so the change is <m>m_1 - m_0</m>. We can continue in this way, so let's say we want to get the change in year 1 to year 2. Well, again, what is that change? Well, it's going to be the population in year 2, <m>m_2</m>, minus the population in year 1, <m>m_1</m>, so it's going to be <m>m_2 - m_1</m>. We can do this (*in) general, let's say let's start with year <m>t</m>, so if the year is <m>t</m>, then what is the next year?  Well that's going to have to be year <m>(t+1)</m>. So the change from year <m>t</m> to year <m>(t+1)</m>, well that's just going to be the population size in year <m>(t+1)</m> (that's the later population size– it's <m>m_{t+1}</m>) minus the previous population size (which is just <m>m_t</m>). So <m>m_{t+1} - m_t</m> is the change from year (*<m>t</m>) to year <m>(t+1)</m>. Well, what do we want that change to be? We want it to be an increase of 8%. Well, ok, so 8% of what? We want it to be 8% of <m>m_t</m>, because that’s the population size we started with. So, we want the change of <m>m_{t+1} - m_t</m> to be 8% of <m>m_t</m> –which is 0.08 times <m>m_t</m>. That is our dynamical rule: the change is <m>0.08 m_t</m>. And that's our dynamical system – and I guess we should add one more thing – we should put back the initial condition: that in year zero, that <m>m_0</m> is 1000 (that's our initial condition.) So, coupled with the initial condition "<m>m_0</m> is 1000", and our rule "<m>m_{t+1} - m_t = 0.08 m_t</m>", we can calculate the moose population in any resulting time – and that's our dynamical system.
</p>

</aside>

<aside><title>Transcript: An introduction to discrete dynamical systems, part 2</title>
<p>Here we have a discrete dynamical system for our moose population. Here, m_t is the number of moose population in year <m>t,</m> and the rule is over here. So we have <m>m_{t+1}</m> (this is the moose population in the next year – in the year <m>(t+1)</m>) minus the previous time population (which is <m>m_t</m>) is 8% or 0.08 times the previous population size, <m>m_t</m>, so that gives us the <em>change</em>. And then we have the initial condition: starting in year 0 we have <m>m_0</m> is 1000, so we start with 1000 moose in year 0. And, if the moose population increases by 8% each year, and that rule is always in effect, then we can use this rule to calculate the moose population in any future time. So, let's see if we can calculate some future moose populations. We have the moose population in year 0, so to calculate the moose population in year 1, we want to use this equation over here, with <m>t</m> equals 0. And if <m>t</m> is equal to 0, then <m>t+1</m> is 1, so we have <m>m_1</m> minus <m>m_t</m> (and <m>t</m> is zero) so we have <m>m_0</m> is 0.08 times <m>m_t</m> (and remember <m>t</m> is 0), so that gives us <m>m_0</m>. Now, we don’t know what <m>m_1</m> is (that’s what we want to figure out), but we do know <m>m_0</m> (<m>m_0</m> is 1000) so we can plug in 1000 for <m>m_0</m>.  With <m>m_1</m> minus – now – 1000 equals 0.08 times 1000, which is 80.  So, this says that the change in the population from year 0 to year 1 is 80, and therefore, since we started in year 0 with 1000 moose, we end up with 1080 moose in year 1. What about the next time step? We know what the population is in year 1, well what about in year 2? Well, to do that, we put <m>t=1</m> in our equation, and when we do that, <m>t+1</m> is 2, so we have the moose population in year 2 minus the moose population in year 1, that change, is 0.08 times the moose population in year 1.  We now know that the moose population in time 1 is 1080, so we can rewrite our equation with 1080 for <m>m_1</m>, and so we get that the change is 0.08 times 1080, which is 86.4. And therefore, our new population size, we have to add that to 1080; and we have that <m>m_2</m> is 1166.4.  In year 2, we’ve gained a fractional moose. I suppose that doesn’t make sense, and if it really happened we’d feel sad for the mother moose that gave birth to four-tenths of a moose. But let’s not worry about fractional moose in our model; but just, understand that we’ll have to round to get something real. We don’t want to round though in our model, as that would make the model too difficult to handle. So let's just keep going, and continue in this rule, and if we kept doing that (not worrying about fractional moose) you could calculate what you would get in the following year. You could say, well, let's look at what <m>m_3</m> is, and plugging in <m>t=2</m>, you get <m>m_3</m> minus <m>m_2</m>. Well, that’s <m>t=2</m>, you get <m>m_3 - m_2</m> is 0.08 times <m>m_2</m> (which we know is 1166.4), and if you do the math, what you’ll do is you’ll calculate that <m>m_3</m> is 1259.712. And we could keep going; we could calculate that <m>m_4</m>…is…well, I’ll use the same rule (I won’t do it but you could do it, and you would get that <m>m_4</m> is 1360.48896.)  Now I told you that you shouldn’t round, so when using a calculator or a computer, you should keep this whole(*entire) number: 1360.48896. But, you don’t have to write all these digits down for your answer on paper. So, on paper it's ok to say – alright the moose population in time 4 is approximately – 1360….(let’s go to one-hundredths)...1360.49. But, later when you want to calculate <m>m_5</m>, you shouldn’t use this (*rounded) number, you should use the full 1360.48896 to keep going.</p>
<p>We can plot our results using a graph, and here we’ll put (use t for the horizontal axis), and we’ll put <m>m</m>, the moose population on the vertical axis. And lets just plot our first four numbers: so here we have year 0, year 1, year 2, year 3, and year 4, … let’s make this be our initial condition 1000, and up here we have 2000 moose, so our initial condition is: at time 0 we have 1000 moose. Now in time 1, we need 1080, so at time point 1, we need to draw a point at about 1080 (something like right here) and then at time 2, well, we have our fractional moose, 1166.4 so … (and we go like this) and then at time 3 we have 1259…(so this is getting a little bit too high, but close enough). At time 4 we have 1360 – approximately – (something like this). So this is supposed to be 1360-ish (oh, that's not 1360, but pretend that that’s 1360 right there). And, so there we go, that's our plot of the solution to our dynamical system of the moose, calculating the first four years from the rule.</p>
</aside>
